<p><strong>Total weekly capacity: 20 hours of work-week time + ~1 hour Sunday batch = ~21 hours.</strong> Saturday is off. The Sunday batch is a deliberate exception because batching mid-week breaks the deep-work block; the trade is one hour of weekend planning for five hours saved on weekdays.</p>

<h3>How the 20 hours actually break down</h3>

<p>For sanity, the typical week of 20 weekday hours allocates roughly:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>~8-10 hours active client work</strong> (Tuesday + Thursday deep blocks + carry-over slots Mon/Wed)</li>
  <li><strong>~3 hours marketing / outreach</strong> (Monday batch, plus the Wed content slot, plus the alt-week newsletter ship)</li>
  <li><strong>~2 hours maintenance + monitoring</strong> across all monthly clients (Wednesday)</li>
  <li><strong>~2 hours admin</strong> (Friday: invoicing, comms, planning)</li>
  <li><strong>~2.5 hours daily admin overhead</strong> (5 &times; 30 min inbox triage)</li>
  <li><strong>~2.5 hours daily comms / shutdown</strong> (5 &times; 30 min)</li>
</ul>

<p>If a week's reality is different (a launch eats Thursday, an emergency claims Wednesday), the rebalancing happens within the 20-hour budget &mdash; not by extending the day.</p>

<h2>Daily cadence</h2>

<p>Every workday is a single <strong>4-hour working block, 9:00am to 1:30pm</strong>, with a 30-minute off-clock break in the middle (12:00&ndash;12:30). Same shape every day; the variable is which deep-work content the middle two-and-a-half hours owns. The bookends &mdash; triage open, shutdown close &mdash; never move.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>9:00 &ndash; 9:30 &middot; Inbox triage (30 min).</strong> Reply to anything urgent, snooze anything that needs a thoughtful response into the comms slot at 1:00, archive everything else. End the block with a clear inbox. Hard cap at 30 minutes &mdash; if I'm still in email at 9:35 something is wrong.</li>
  <li><strong>9:30 &ndash; 12:00 &middot; Deep work block, part 1 (2.5 hours).</strong> The single most valuable block of the day. Whatever the highest-leverage thing is &mdash; client build work, content draft, outreach batch &mdash; owns it. Email closed, phone in another room, no ad-hoc meetings, no context-switching. This block is what the entire 4-hour day is built around; protect it like a load-bearing wall.</li>
  <li><strong>12:00 &ndash; 12:30 &middot; Real break (30 min, off the clock).</strong> Lunch and a walk away from the screen. Phone in pocket, no podcasts, just the time to actually think. Most of the blog post angles I write come out of this walk; that is not an accident.</li>
  <li><strong>12:30 &ndash; 1:00 &middot; Deep work block, part 2 (30 min).</strong> Continue the morning's deep-work task to a clean stopping point, or pick up the day's secondary deep task if the morning slot finished cleanly. Same rules as part 1: no email, no ad-hoc meetings.</li>
  <li><strong>1:00 &ndash; 1:30 &middot; Client comms + shutdown (30 min).</strong> Respond to DMs, social comments, slower-thread emails. Scheduled discovery calls sit here when booked. Last 10 minutes of the slot: open the day's task list, mark everything done that's done, pull tomorrow's top three items into a small written list, close every browser tab. Walk away with a clean state. Skipping the shutdown is what turns a 4-hour day into a 6-hour scramble.</li>
</ul>

<p>1:30pm onward is not agency time. The day is closed. 4 hours billable, 30 minutes off-clock for lunch, total wall-clock window 4.5 hours.</p>

<p>The shape <em>is</em> the discipline. A 4-hour daily ceiling only works if every day uses the same template; the moment exceptions become routine, "4 hours" becomes 5, then 6, and the constraint that protected the rest of life is gone.</p>

<h2>Holiday + PTO map</h2>

<p>Recurring closures, with the prep windows that precede each:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Thanksgiving week (US):</strong> closed Wednesday through Friday. Auto-responder activates Tuesday evening with the return date and the emergency-protocol link from <a href="/sop/emergencies/">phase 10</a>. Prep window: the Friday before, draft the auto-responder copy and confirm scheduled posts cover the week.</li>
  <li><strong>Late December:</strong> closed December 24 through January 2. Auto-responder activates December 23 evening. Prep window: first week of December, finalize closure dates with each active client, queue any posts that should publish during the closure, batch any year-end client communications.</li>
  <li><strong>Independence Day week (US):</strong> closed July 3 through July 5 if it falls on a weekday. Standard auto-responder.</li>
  <li><strong>Memorial Day, Labor Day:</strong> closed Monday only. Auto-responder activates Friday evening.</li>
  <li><strong>Per-year PTO:</strong> two weeks total, taken in 3-5 day chunks. Each chunk has a backup contact arrangement (named in advance) for genuine emergencies and a longer auto-responder window than the holidays use.</li>
</ul>

<p>The auto-responder copy and the PTO handoff checklist both live in <a href="/sop/email-templates/">email templates</a> and <a href="/sop/checklists/">checklists</a> respectively.</p>

<h2>What feeds what</h2>

<p>The cadences nest into each other. The flow, top of funnel to delivery:</p>

<pre>cold outreach (Monday)            local network (monthly events)
       │                                    │
       └──────► discovery calls ◄───────────┘
                     │
                     ▼
              proposal (within 24h)
                     │
                     ▼
              agreement signed + first invoice
                     │
                     ▼
              onboarding (week 1) ──► design (week 2-3)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      build (week 3-5)
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      pre-launch QA + launch
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      monthly maintenance ◄── runs forever
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      quarterly QBR
                                         │
                                         ▼
                                      annual review + rate notice</pre>

<p>The marketing cadences (content, newsletter, social, partners, reviews) run in parallel, all feeding into "discovery calls booked" as the proximal output. The lifecycle cadences (onboarding through offboarding) process the engagements that result.</p>

